---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { personalData } from '../data/personal.js';
---

<Layout title="About — Aaditya Shah" description="About Aaditya Shah, his method, interests, and longer horizon.">
  <Header />
  <main id="main-content">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1>Questions start the work. Building tells me what I understand.</h1>
        <p>I am Aaditya Shah, a second-year B.Tech student in Computer Science and Engineering at IIT Gandhinagar, graduating in 2029. I am drawn to difficult questions at the meeting point of mathematics, computation, and engineered systems.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell about-grid">
        <aside class="about-aside">
          <p class="eyebrow">Current focus</p>
          <p>Mathematical foundations<br />Enigma cryptanalysis<br />Exploring quantum computing<br />Algorithms and data structures<br />Interactive systems as a medium<br />Design and collaboration<br />Philosophy of knowledge and mind</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="about-copy">
          <p class="about-lede">The common thread is not a fixed technology stack. It is curiosity: trying to understand why something works, what structure is hiding underneath, whether the explanation can be generalized, and what building it reveals.</p>
          <p>I entered IIT Gandhinagar in Mechanical Engineering and changed to Computer Science after my first semester. I was certain that I wanted to move closer to computation; I was much less certain about what I wanted computation to lead toward. That uncertainty now feels less like a problem to eliminate and more like room to investigate.</p>
          <p>Programming has been an important part of how I learn. I have built Android apps, simulations, web experiences, and small automated systems, but I increasingly see software as a medium rather than a professional identity: a way to make an idea concrete enough to test, share, or challenge.</p>
          <p>Right now, the Enigma investigation is my main research thread. I am also beginning to study quantum computing through its mathematical foundations, learning algorithms more deliberately, and reading philosophy—especially questions about knowledge, consciousness, ethics, and what it means to understand something.</p>
          <p>I have a habit of following questions further than I originally intended. Sometimes that becomes research; sometimes it becomes a prototype, a page of mathematics, or simply a better question.</p>
          <p>Building with other people has widened that process. At Blithchron, I serve as a Design and Tech Coordinator and have deployed the <a href="https://blithchron.iitgn.ac.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Blithchron 2027 website</a>. The work keeps reminding me that understanding a system also means understanding the people, constraints, and decisions around it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section philosophy-section">
      <div class="shell philosophy-grid">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Why this atlas</p><h2 class="section-title">Make the work understandable.</h2></div>
        <div class="philosophy-copy">
          <p>I wanted this site to behave more like a working notebook than a polished résumé.</p>
          <p>Questions can remain unfinished. Investigations can show failed assumptions and changed conclusions. A project can matter even when I no longer want it to define me.</p>
          <p><strong>The aim is not to make everything look impressive. It is to leave an honest, useful record of what I was trying to understand.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section method-section">
      <div class="shell">
        <header class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">How I work</p><h2 class="section-title">A question becomes clearer by moving through a few forms.</h2><p class="section-intro">Every investigation can inherit this method, even when the order bends or a step sends me back to an earlier one.</p></div></header>
        <ol class="method-list">
          <li><span class="index-number">01</span><strong>Notice</strong><p>Find the thing that does not yet make sense.</p></li>
          <li><span class="index-number">02</span><strong>Read</strong><p>Locate the history, language, and existing explanations.</p></li>
          <li><span class="index-number">03</span><strong>Model</strong><p>Make the structure explicit enough to reason about.</p></li>
          <li><span class="index-number">04</span><strong>Build</strong><p>Turn an idea into a system, simulation, or artifact.</p></li>
          <li><span class="index-number">05</span><strong>Test</strong><p>Look for evidence, counterexamples, and constraints.</p></li>
          <li><span class="index-number">06</span><strong>Explain</strong><p>Write down what changed and what still resists clarity.</p></li>
          <li><span class="index-number">07</span><strong>Return</strong><p>Come back when a better question or method appears.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell horizon">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Long horizon</p><blockquote>The horizon is larger than software.</blockquote></div>
        <div class="horizon-copy">
          <p>Over time, I want to move closer to technologies that change what humans can understand or build—especially quantum systems, intelligence, robotics, energy, and computation that meets the physical world.</p>
          <p>These are directions of attention, not a settled ten-year plan. For now, the work is to build strong foundations, follow difficult questions carefully, and notice which of them continue to hold my attention after the novelty disappears.</p>
          <p>This atlas is meant to grow with that process: notes can become investigations, investigations can become research or tools, and questions can be revised without pretending they were ever settled.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell horizon">
        <blockquote>Working on something difficult?</blockquote>
        <div class="horizon-copy">
          <p>I am always glad to hear from people thinking carefully about mathematics, computation, research, or complex systems.</p>
          <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href={`mailto:${personalData.socialLinks.mail}`}>Write to me ↗</a><a class="button secondary" href={personalData.socialLinks.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
